Philological study: defend a reading of the Greek text (@greek-philological-study: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-scholarly-path, #source @greek-source-smyth, #source @greek-source-plato-critical, #source @greek-source-aristotle-critical, #source @greek-source-plotinus-critical, #philosophy @greek-scholarly-literature, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-scholarly-literature) { r_353E4E4WH3BWFHHJNZMPKBPEXF
**Preparation:** start with [the original-language entrance](../languages/Read-original-texts.lingua#r_1Q07KPDNG2JTY7HRWQY5H6T5MJ) and continue until you can analyse the passages your argument uses. [Smyth's grammar](../sources/Greek-Grammar-Smyth.lingua#r_1HWQDW2XAJNQAXTWS9P0QT57DG) supplies a substantial reference beyond the introductory textbook. The required ability concerns reading and defending selected Greek, with appropriate aids, rather than claiming unaided fluency in the entire corpus.

Choose a coherent argumentative passage, an identified [critical Greek edition of Plato](../sources/Plato-critical-editions.lingua#r_28N1ADM4R3DAG5K2898WPKRXCK) or [Aristotle](../sources/Metaphysics-Jaeger.lingua#r_2MJF84NGSE83J9KWD3B8KBNPZ1), and two identified translations where useful. For Plotinus, use [the corresponding critical edition](../sources/Plotinus-Henry-Schwyzer.lingua#r_3EYTA95ETXPPZFHSN5RJ5Q5EM4). Read the passage within its full argument before extracting a sentence.

**Produce a textual dossier:**

1. Identify work, edition, editor, passage, and the citation system: Stephanus, Bekker, treatise/chapter/section, or the source collection's own numbering.
2. Supply your translation and analyse the syntax, important word senses, connections, and scope of claims. Explain the grammatical choices your interpretation depends on.
3. Compare translations and distinguish lexical, syntactic, and philosophical differences.
4. Read the relevant apparatus entries and editorial signs. If a textual variant affects your argument, explain the competing readings and why the adopted text matters. If the argument does not turn on a variant, explain what you checked.
5. Identify the quoted passage's transmitting author or dramatic setting, and distinguish direct text from testimony and editorial reconstruction.
6. State the limits of the evidence and the aids used.

Manuscript collation and palaeography become preparation when a project depends on a claim about witnesses; the first dossier can work with a responsibly used published critical apparatus. A dictionary gloss or an automated translation does not by itself defend the syntax or interpretation.

**Check:** a reader competent in the selected text can challenge the translation and trace your response to the Greek and edition. Revise the dossier after criticism. Apply it within an [advanced seminar](seminars/Seminars.lingua#r_5FTSQDJTR924S9Z0G9EDBQFR1Y) and the [research project](Independent-research.lingua#r_08YBJ1G76KSJ3QTG42FKCCAV5K).

